  "outcome": "answer",
  "answer_summary": "Operating margin fell 1.37 pp (137 bps) (16.32% → 14.95%). Management cites wage and benefit investments, deleverage and promotions, partly offset by pricing and efficiencies, but its quantified drivers net to only -60 bps. The income statement shows store operating expenses as the largest drag (-1.338 pp). The non-recurrence of a fiscal 2023 asset-sale gain (-0.254 pp) goes unmentioned. Wages and benefits barely moved as a share of revenue (+0.13 pp (+13 bps)). The explanation is therefore only partially supported.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "c1",
      "type": "management_assertion",
      "text": "Management attributes the 130 bps operating-margin contraction primarily to investments in store partner wages and benefits (~140 bps), deleverage (~130 bps) and increased promotional activity (~100 bps), partially offset by pricing (~180 bps) and in-store operational efficiencies (~130 bps).",
      "support": "supported",
      "citation_ids": [
        "e21"
      ],
      "calculation_ids": [],
      "verification_note": "'Supported' here means the filing says this; whether the evidence supports the explanation is c6."
    },
    {
      "id": "c2",
      "type": "calculated",
      "text": "Operating margin fell 1.37 pp (137 bps), from 16.32% to 14.95%.",
      "support": "supported",
      "citation_ids": [
        "e20",
        "e19",
        "e2",
        "e1"
      ],
      "calculation_ids": [
        "k2",
        "k3",
        "k4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c3",
      "type": "calculated",
      "text": "Management's quantified drivers net to -60 bps, so about 70 bps of the stated 130 bps contraction is not accounted for by the listed items.",
      "support": "supported",
      "citation_ids": [
        "e21"
      ],
      "calculation_ids": [
        "k5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c4",
      "type": "calculated",
      "text": "Store operating expenses rose from 40.92% to 42.26% of total net revenues, lowering margin by 1.338 pp — the largest single contributor. Product and distribution costs fell as a share of revenue, raising margin by 0.807 pp.",
      "support": "supported",
      "citation_ids": [
        "e5",
        "e6",
        "e3",
        "e4"
      ],
      "calculation_ids": [
        "k1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c5",
      "type": "calculated",
      "text": "Fiscal 2023 included a $91.3 million gain from sale of assets with no fiscal 2024 equivalent; its absence lowered the margin comparison by 0.254 pp. Management's driver list does not mention it.",
      "support": "supported",
      "citation_ids": [
        "e18",
        "e21"
      ],
      "calculation_ids": [
        "k1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c6",
      "type": "system_inference",
      "text": "Management's explanation is only partially supported. Its direction — store-level cost pressure — is consistent with the rise in store operating expenses. But the size of individual drivers can't be verified from reported line items: total wages and benefits moved only +0.13 pp (+13 bps) as a share of revenue, against a stated ~140 bps impact. The attribution is likely measured before the offsetting efficiencies, on a basis the filing does not disclose. The filing can't establish that these factors caused the whole decline.",
      "support": "partially_supported",
      "citation_ids": [
        "e21",
        "e22",
        "e23"
      ],
      "calculation_ids": [
        "k1",
        "k5",
        "k8"
      ]
    }
  ],
